<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bagrationi dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bagratids (Georgia), Georgian Bagratids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bagrationi were the royal dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, one of the longest-ruling families in the world. Rising in Tao-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klarjeti">Klarjeti</a> in the 9th century, they united Georgia under Bagrat III in the early 11th century and reached their height under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> IV and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamar-of-georgia">Queen Tamar</a>. After the kingdom split in the 15th century, branches ruled Kartli, Kakheti and Imereti until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> annexed them in 1801 and 1810.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bagrationi-dynasty.jpg" alt="Bagrationi dynasty" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: from here · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bagrationi_dynasty_Coat_of_Arms.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The origins of the Bagrationi are disputed. Georgian scholars argue they were of Georgian origin, while Armenian and Western scholars see them as a branch of the Armenian Bagratuni dynasty. A tradition first recorded by the 11th-century chronicler Sumbat Davitis-Dze held that the family descended from the biblical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">King David</a> and came from Israel around 530. Modern scholarship has discounted this. Cyril Toumanoff concluded that the Georgian line began with Adarnase, who passed to Kartli after a failed rising against Arab rule in 775, and whose son Ashot I acquired the principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> in 813. The legend of Davidic descent later helped maintain the dynasty's legitimacy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The sons and grandsons of Ashot I founded three branches, Kartli, Tao and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klarjeti">Klarjeti</a>, which fought each other and their neighbours. The Kartli line prevailed, and in 888 Adarnase I restored the Iberian royal authority that had lain dormant since 580. Bagrat III consolidated his inheritance in Tao-Klarjeti and Abkhazia with the help of his foster-father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-iii-kuropalates">David III of Tao</a>. The united monarchy flourished under David IV the Builder (1089–1125), who finished the unification by retaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> in 1122, and under his great-granddaughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamar-of-georgia">Tamar the Great</a> (1184–1213). When the male line ended with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a>, the dynasty continued through Tamar's marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-soslan">David Soslan</a>, an Alan prince of reputed Bagratid descent.</p><p>The Khwarezmians in 1225 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in 1236 ended the golden age. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s eight invasions between 1386 and 1403 dealt a heavy blow, and by 1490/91 the kingdom had split into three: Kartli, Kakheti and Imereti, each under a branch of the family. For three centuries they lived under Ottoman and Persian overlordship, and many converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> to be invested by their suzerains.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erekle-ii">Erekle II</a> in 1798 and of his son George XII, Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-i-of-russia">Paul I of Russia</a> declared Kartli-Kakheti annexed on 18 December 1800, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> formally deposed the dynasty on 12 September 1801. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon-ii-of-imereti">Solomon II of Imereti</a> was dethroned by Russian forces on 20 February 1810 and died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trabzon</a> in 1815. Most Bagrationi princes who resisted were arrested and deported.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> the family became prominent aristocrats, among them Prince Pyotr Bagration, a general and hero of the war of 1812. The dynasty remained a Russian noble family until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a> of 1917. After Soviet rule was established in 1921 some members lost property, others moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>, and some returned after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> regained independence in 1991.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bagrationi dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Russia, protector by the Treaty of Georgievsk of 1783, gave no help when Iran sacked Tbilisi in 1795, then annexed eastern Georgia in 1801, abolishing the <b>Bagrationi dynasty</b> and the autocephaly of the Georgian Church.</blockquote><a href="/empire/georgia">Georgia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the south-west the <b>Bagratid</b> princes of Tao-Klarjeti revived the title of king of the Iberians in 888, while in the west the Kingdom of Abkhazia, ruled from Kutaisi, had become a Georgian-speaking Orthodox realm.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Bagrat, a prince of a junior <b>Bagratid</b> line who governed in the west, made himself king of Imereti, defeated King George VIII at the Battle of Chikhori in 1463 and took Kartli as well in 1465.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-imereti">Kingdom of Imereti · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1465, amid the disintegration of the Kingdom of Georgia, George I, a prince of the royal <b>Bagratid</b> house, made Kakheti a separate kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kakheti">Kingdom of Kakheti</a></li><li><blockquote>When George died in 1800, Emperor Paul I and then Alexander I annexed the kingdom in 1801, deposing the <b>Bagratid</b> dynasty and making it a Russian province.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kartli-kakheti">Kingdom of Kartli-Kakheti</a></li><li><blockquote>Early in the 9th century the <b>Bagratid</b> prince Ashot I moved from Kartli to the south-western borderland of Klarjeti, where he rebuilt Artanuji and the family won Byzantine titles.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-iberians">Kingdom of the Iberians</a></li></ul></section>
